course: biology
lesson: 72
title: Staying healthy: germs, vaccines, sleep, food and exercise
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Your body is a castle under siege by germs too small to see, and it has walls (skin), guards (the immune system), repair teams (platelets) and a thermostat it can turn up to fight back.
explain: Singing Happy Birthday twice while you wash with soap is not a silly rule: it gives the soap about 20 seconds to grab the grease where germs hide and let the water rinse them away.
explain: A vaccine is a wanted poster for a germ: the immune system studies a harmless copy, remembers it, and destroys the real germ fast if it ever turns up.
explain: Cartoons show germs as green monsters you can see, cold weather giving you a cold, and every pill curing everything; in real life colds come from viruses, antibiotics only kill bacteria, and plain soap, sleep, food and play do most of the work.
fact: Soap grabs grease and dirt where germs hide so water can rinse them off, and it breaks the fatty coat of some viruses; in the 1840s Ignaz Semmelweis saved many mothers' lives by making doctors wash their hands.
fact: A sneeze shoots droplets that can travel a few metres, and sneezing into the elbow keeps germs off the hands that touch pencils, door handles and friends.
fact: A vaccine shows the immune system a harmless copy or piece of a germ, and memory cells then destroy the real germ fast; Edward Jenner made the first vaccine with cowpox in 1796, and vaccines helped wipe out smallpox by 1980.
fact: Colds are caused by viruses, most often rhinoviruses; antibiotics do nothing against viruses, and going out with wet hair does not give you a cold.
fact: When you graze your knee, platelets plug the wound and threads of fibrin make a net that dries into a scab, a natural plaster under which new skin grows; picking it can let germs in and leave a scar.
fact: Normal body temperature is about 37 degrees Celsius; in a fever the brain turns the thermostat up to make life harder for germs, and shivering is the body heating itself to the new setting.
fact: No single food has everything the body needs, so a balanced meal gives protein for building, carbohydrates for energy, some fat, vitamins, minerals and fibre; sailors once got scurvy from lack of vitamin C until lemons and limes were added to their food.
fact: Bacteria in plaque eat sugar and make acid that dissolves enamel into cavities; brushing twice a day for two minutes with fluoride toothpaste helps, and brushing at bedtime matters most because bacteria feed on sugar left in the mouth all night, and enamel, the hardest substance in the body, cannot grow back.
fact: Exercise makes the heart pump faster and the lungs breathe harder; the heart gets stronger with use, running and jumping make bones stronger, and children should be active for about an hour a day on average.
fact: The body is about 60 percent water; sweat cools the skin as it dries, and pale yellow wee is a simple sign you are drinking enough.
fact: Ultraviolet rays in sunlight damage skin cells and cause sunburn; sunscreen blocks much of it but must be put on again after swimming, and you can burn on a cloudy day, because most ultraviolet rays pass through thin cloud.
fact: In 1928 Alexander Fleming noticed that a mould, Penicillium, had killed bacteria on a dish, which led to penicillin; antibiotics only work on bacteria, and taking them when not needed helps bacteria become resistant.
exercise: How long should you rub your hands with soap? | About 20 seconds, two rounds of Happy Birthday | About 2 seconds, just a quick splash under the tap | About 5 minutes, until your hands wrinkle | No time at all, water alone is enough
exercise: How does soap help get rid of germs? | It grabs grease where germs hide so water rinses them off | It makes the germs fall asleep on your skin so they cannot hurt you | It glues the germs to your hands so they cannot move | It only works if the water is boiling hot
exercise: Which doctor saved many mothers' lives in the 1840s by making doctors wash their hands? | Ignaz Semmelweis | Edward Jenner, the country doctor | Alexander Fleming | Charles Darwin, the naturalist
exercise: Why sneeze into the bend of your elbow? | It keeps germs off your hands, which touch everything | Elbows kill germs with a special kind of skin found only there | It makes the sneeze louder and stronger | It stops you from catching your own cold
exercise: How does a vaccine protect you? | It teaches the immune system to know a germ using a harmless copy | It kills every germ in your body for ten years | It covers your blood with a thin germproof coat that lasts all your life | It gives you the illness quickly so it is over
exercise: Who made the first vaccine, using cowpox, in 1796? | Edward Jenner | Alexander Fleming | Louis Braille, the teacher | Mary Anning, the fossil hunter
exercise: What causes a cold? | A virus, most often a rhinovirus | Going outside with wet hair | Eating ice cream in winter | Sitting near an open window
exercise: Why do antibiotics not cure a cold? | Antibiotics kill bacteria, and colds are caused by viruses | Colds are far too strong for any medicine that doctors have | Antibiotics only work in summer | Antibiotics only work on adults
exercise: What makes a scab form on a grazed knee? | Platelets plug the wound and fibrin threads make a net | Dirt from the playground dries into a hard crust over the cut | Skin from elsewhere slides over the hole | Sweat dries into a hard brown lid
exercise: What is normal body temperature? | About 37 degrees Celsius | About 30 degrees Celsius | About 42 degrees Celsius | About 100 degrees Celsius
exercise: What illness did sailors get from a lack of vitamin C? | Scurvy | Smallpox | Chickenpox | A common cold
exercise: What makes holes in teeth? | Acid made by bacteria in plaque that eat sugar | Brushing your teeth too often and too hard every day | Drinking too much cold water | Chewing hard foods like carrots
exercise: Which is the hardest substance in the human body? | Tooth enamel | Thigh bone | Fingernail | Skull bone
exercise: How long should children be active each day, on average? | At least about an hour | About five minutes | Only on weekends | Exactly three hours, never less
exercise: About how much of the body is water? | About 60 percent | About 10 percent, very little | About 95 percent, like a jellyfish | About 30 percent, a third
exercise: Why put sunscreen on again after swimming? | Water washes it off, and ultraviolet still reaches the skin | Sunscreen only works while your skin is still wet from the pool | Swimming makes the sun hotter | It doesn't matter, it lasts all week
exercise: What did Alexander Fleming notice in 1928? | A mould had killed the bacteria around it on a dish | A cow had cured a girl of smallpox by licking her hand | Doctors' dirty hands spread illness | Lemons stopped sailors getting scurvy
test: «it's freezing and my hair's wet, Mum says I'll catch a cold» What is right? | You only catch a cold from a virus, often passed on by someone | Yes, cold wet hair turns into a real cold | Yes, but only if it is below zero | No, colds only come from eating sweets
test: «just give me antibiotics for this cold, it'll be gone tomorrow» What is right? | Antibiotics do nothing to cold viruses, and needless use helps bacteria resist | Yes, antibiotics kill every germ there is, including the viruses that cause colds | Yes, but only if you take double the dose | No, antibiotics only work on broken bones
test: In a cartoon, green germ monsters with faces jump off a door handle. What is true? | Real germs are far too small to see without a microscope | Germs are green and about the size of an ant, so you can squash them | Germs only live on door handles | Germs are always harmful and must all be killed
test: «why am I shivering?? my head's boiling» What is right? | The brain turned the thermostat up, and shivering makes heat to reach it | Shivering means the fever has already gone and you are getting better now | Your body is too cold and needs a cold bath | Shivering means the germs are winning for sure
test: «gonna pick my scab, it's well itchy» What is the best answer? | Leave it: picking can let germs in and leave a scar | Pick it, the wound heals much faster with the scab off and air on it | Pick it and put soil on to dry it | Pick it, scabs are only dried dirt
test: What would happen if you only ate chips for months? | You would miss vitamins, because no single food has everything | Nothing, chips have every vitamin and mineral that a growing child needs | You would grow faster than other children | You would never get ill again
test: «had a fizzy drink and a sweet, too tired to brush, whatever» Why is brushing at bedtime especially important? | Bacteria feed on sugar all night and make acid that eats enamel | Teeth grow at night and need cleaning | Toothpaste helps you fall asleep | Saliva is stronger at night and protects teeth, so brushing matters less in the morning
test: «it's cloudy, don't need sunscreen» What is right? | Most ultraviolet rays pass through thin clouds, so you can still burn | Clouds block every single ultraviolet ray completely, so you are safe | Sunburn only happens when you feel hot | Ultraviolet only reaches the beach at noon
test: «bloody hot match, I'm parched, how do I know I've drunk enough» What is a simple sign? | Pale yellow wee | Dark brown, smelly wee | Not needing the toilet all day | A headache and a dry mouth
test: «my mate says the jab gives you the actual disease» What is right? | A vaccine uses a harmless copy or piece of the germ, not the disease | Yes, a vaccine is the full disease in a small dose, so you get ill a bit | Yes, but only for one week | No, a vaccine is just sugar water
test: Why do you puff and go red after running round the playground? | The heart pumps faster and the lungs work harder to bring oxygen | The body is overheating and is just about to stop working altogether | Running squeezes all the blood into your face | The lungs are running out of water
exam: Identify the principal reason for which antibiotic medicines are ineffective against the common cold. | The common cold is caused by viruses, whereas antibiotics act upon bacteria | The common cold is caused by bacteria too resistant for any medicine | Antibiotics are effective only at elevated temperatures | The common cold is caused by exposure to cold air rather than by any germ
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the mode of action of a vaccine? | It presents a harmless copy or portion of a germ, such that the immune system retains a memory of it | It eliminates all micro-organisms from the body for a period of ten years | It induces the full disease in a brief and controlled form | It forms an impermeable coating over the surface of the blood
exam: Ascertain the normal temperature of the human body, expressed in degrees Celsius. | Approximately thirty-seven | Approximately thirty | Approximately forty-two | Approximately one hundred
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the observation made by Alexander Fleming in the year 1928. | A Penicillium mould had destroyed the bacteria around it upon a culture dish | Cowpox had conferred protection against smallpox among those who milked cows | The washing of hands had reduced deaths among mothers | Citrus fruit had prevented scurvy among sailors
